# The Britons in Brittany: the long read (story-brittany.html), its map, its timeline events and two new people.
# It takes the place of the short "Wales and Brittany" read that was in pride3.py, at the same address, so every
# link to story-brittany.html still works. features.py adds STORY; data.py adds EVENTS; people2.py adds PEOPLE;
# build.py draws the map from PINS (brittany_map()) and src/geo/brittany.json (made by tools/brittany_map.py).
# Asked for by Ifan, 13 November 2026: "A page on our migrations to Brittany during Macsen Wledig and during the
# Anglo Saxon invasion of Britain".
# House rules (EDITORIAL.md): proud; nothing negative about the people of Welsh history; the Britons and the Cymry,
# never "Celts"; kings are kings; Welsh tradition told in its own words ("Welsh tradition holds", "the tale tells").
# The Dream of Macsen's tale of the women's tongues is left out (it is in the tale; it adds nothing but gore).
# Quotations, each checked word for word, November 2026:
#   Gildas, De Excidio 14 and 25, tr. J. A. Giles (Project Gutenberg ebook 1949).
#   Historia Brittonum 27, tr. J. A. Giles (as in src/macsen.py).
#   Geoffrey of Monmouth, Historia Regum Britanniae V.12, V.15, V.16, tr. Sebastian Evans (1904).
#   Jordanes, Getica 237, tr. Charles C. Mierow (1915).
# Facts checked November 2026: Council of Tours 461 (Mansuetus, episcopus Britannorum, last of eight to sign);
#   Sidonius Apollinaris, Letters III.9, to Riothamus; Gregory of Tours, Histories II.18 (Britanni driven from Bourges,
#   many killed at Déols); Life of Samson (Amon of Dyfed, Anna of Gwent, Illtud, Caldey, Cornwall, Dol, Childebert,
#   Iudual; written between 610 and 820); Wrmonoc's Life of Paul Aurelian, 884 (Penychen, Illtud, Lampaul on Ushant,
#   Saint-Pol, Batz); Book of Llandaff, Life of Teilo (Dol, seven years and seven months, the orchard; Landeleau and
#   Saint-Thélo); Wrdisten's Life of Winwaloe (Fracan and Gwen, Budoc, Landévennec); the seven founder saints and the
#   Tro Breizh (about 600 km, relaunched 1994); Lanildut "from lann and Ildut" (Patrimoine Iroise); Thomas Price
#   (Carnhuanawc) and Le Gonidec's New Testament, 1827; the Gorsedd of Brittany (twenty Bretons received at the Gorsedd
#   of Wales, Cardiff, 1899; first assembly Guingamp, 1 September 1900); Bro Gozh ma Zadoù (Taldir, 1897; adopted at
#   Lesneven, 1903; tune of James James); Breton speakers 107,000 (2024); Diwan 1977.
# Paragraph codes (see prose() in build.py): "## Heading", "> Quote | Source", "|| Left | Right", "!! key" (a picture).

