PEOPLE = [
 P("riothamus", "saints", "fl. c. 469", "Riothamus", "Riothamus", "King of the Britons who fought in Gaul", "Brenin y Brythoniaid a ymladdodd yng Ngâl", "Riothamus", "rulers",
   "King of the Britons who crossed the sea with twelve thousand men, about 469, to fight for Rome against the Visigoths in the heart of Gaul. Sidonius Apollinaris wrote to him as a friend.",
   "Brenin y Brythoniaid a groesodd y môr gyda deuddeng mil o wŷr, tua 469, i ymladd dros Rufain yn erbyn y Fisigothiaid yng nghalon Gâl. Ysgrifennodd Sidonius Apollinaris ato fel cyfaill.",
   ["Riothamus is the first king of the Britons after the Romans whose deeds were written down by others in his own lifetime. His name, Rigotamos in the Brittonic of his day, means the greatest king, the kingliest.",
    "In the late 460s the emperor Anthemius was struggling to hold Roman Gaul against Euric, king of the Visigoths, and he called on the Britons for help. Jordanes, who wrote the history of the Goths in about 551, tells that ‘their King Riotimus came with twelve thousand men into the state of the Bituriges by the way of Ocean’: by sea and on into the country around Bourges, in the very middle of Gaul.",
    "Euric came against him with a great army before the Romans could join him, and after a long fight the Britons were beaten. Gregory of Tours remembered that many of them fell at the village of Déols. Riothamus withdrew with the men he had left to the Burgundians, the allies of Rome.",
    "One letter to him survives. Sidonius Apollinaris, poet and bishop of Clermont, wrote ‘to his friend Riothamus’, asking him to hear the case of a humble man of the district and judge it fairly. It shows a king of the Britons holding court in Gaul and treated as an equal by the greatest Roman writer of the age.",
    "Where his kingdom lay, the sources do not say. His men may have come from Britain itself, or from the Britons already settled in Armorica, the land that was becoming Llydaw; either way they were Britons, and they crossed the sea as one people. His story is told in our long read on how the Britons made a second Britain in Brittany."],
   ["Riothamus yw’r brenin cyntaf ar y Brythoniaid ar ôl y Rhufeiniaid y cofnodwyd ei weithredoedd gan eraill yn ystod ei oes ei hun. Ystyr ei enw, Rigotamos ym Mrythoneg ei oes, yw’r brenin mwyaf, y brenhinolaf.",
    "Ar ddiwedd y 460au roedd yr ymerawdwr Anthemius yn ymdrechu i ddal Gâl Rufeinig yn erbyn Euric, brenin y Fisigothiaid, a galwodd ar y Brythoniaid am gymorth. Medd Jordanes, a ysgrifennodd hanes y Gothiaid tua 551, i’w ‘Brenin Riotimus ddod gyda deuddeng mil o wŷr i wlad y Bituriges ar hyd y Cefnfor’: dros y môr ac ymlaen i’r wlad o gwmpas Bourges, ym mherfedd Gâl.",
    "Daeth Euric yn ei erbyn â byddin fawr cyn i’r Rhufeiniaid allu ymuno ag ef, ac ar ôl ymladd hir trechwyd y Brythoniaid. Cofiai Gregori o Tours i lawer ohonynt syrthio ym mhentref Déols. Enciliodd Riothamus gyda’r gwŷr a oedd ganddo ar ôl at y Bwrgwyniaid, cynghreiriaid Rhufain.",
    "Mae un llythyr ato wedi goroesi. Ysgrifennodd Sidonius Apollinaris, bardd ac esgob Clermont, ‘at ei gyfaill Riothamus’, yn gofyn iddo wrando achos gŵr distadl o’r ardal a’i farnu’n deg. Mae’n dangos brenin y Brythoniaid yn cynnal llys yng Ngâl ac yn cael ei drin fel cydradd gan lenor Rhufeinig mwyaf yr oes.",
    "Nid yw’r ffynonellau’n dweud ble’r oedd ei deyrnas. Efallai i’w wŷr ddod o Brydain ei hun, neu o’r Brythoniaid a oedd eisoes wedi ymsefydlu yn Armorica, y wlad a oedd yn dod yn Llydaw; y naill ffordd neu’r llall, Brythoniaid oeddynt, a chroesasant y môr yn un bobl. Adroddir ei hanes yn ein herthygl ar sut y gwnaeth y Brythoniaid ail Brydain yn Llydaw."]),
 P("gwenole", "saints", "fl. c. 500", "Saint Gwenole", "Sant Gwenole", "Founder of Landévennec, son of a Briton", "Sylfaenydd Landevenneg, mab i Frython", "Winwaloe", "faith",
   "Born in Brittany to Fracan and Gwen, Britons who had crossed from Dumnonia. He founded the abbey of Landévennec, the great house of learning of Brittany, and is honoured in Cornwall and Wales too.",
   "Ganwyd yn Llydaw i Fracan a Gwen, Brythoniaid a groesodd o Ddumnonia. Sefydlodd abaty Landevenneg, tŷ dysg mawr Llydaw, ac fe’i hanrhydeddir yng Nghernyw a Chymru hefyd.",
   ["Gwenole, Winwaloe or Guénolé, was a child of the great crossing. His Life, written at Landévennec by the monk Wrdisten in the ninth century, tells that his father Fracan was a Briton of Dumnonia who crossed the sea with his wife Gwen and their sons Gwethenoc and Jacut, and settled in the north of Armorica, where the village of Ploufragan still carries Fracan’s name. Gwenole was born in Armorica and grew up there.",
    "As a boy he was given to the holy man Budoc, who kept a school on the island of Lavret, off Bréhat. With eleven companions he went west, first to the island of Tibidy, and then across the water to the estuary of the Aulne, where he founded the abbey of Landévennec.",
    "Landévennec became the great house of learning of Brittany. Its monks wrote the Lives of the saints who had come from Britain, among them Wrmonoc’s Life of Paul Aurelian. Gwenole is one of the best loved of the Breton saints, and a statue of him carved in 1522 is still kept at his abbey.",
    "His family belonged to both shores of the sea. In Welsh tradition his mother, Gwen Teirbron, was also the mother of Cadfan, who came from Llydaw to found Tywyn and the monastery on Ynys Enlli. In Cornwall Gwenole is the saint of Gunwalloe and Landewednack, and in Wales two lost chapels and perhaps the churches of St Twynnells in Pembrokeshire and Wonastow in Gwent were his."],
   ["Plentyn y croesi mawr oedd Gwenole, Winwaloe neu Guénolé. Medd ei Fuchedd, a ysgrifennwyd yn Landevenneg gan y mynach Wrdisten yn y nawfed ganrif, fod ei dad Fracan yn Frython o Ddumnonia a groesodd y môr gyda’i wraig Gwen a’u meibion Gwethenoc a Jacut, ac ymsefydlu yng ngogledd Armorica, lle mae pentref Ploufragan yn dwyn enw Fracan o hyd. Yn Armorica y ganwyd Gwenole, ac yno y magwyd ef.",
    "Yn fachgen fe’i rhoddwyd i’r gŵr sanctaidd Budoc, a gadwai ysgol ar ynys Lavret, ger Bréhat. Gydag un ar ddeg o gymdeithion aeth tua’r gorllewin, yn gyntaf i ynys Tibidy, ac yna dros y dŵr i aber afon Aulne, lle sefydlodd abaty Landevenneg.",
    "Daeth Landevenneg yn dŷ dysg mawr Llydaw. Ysgrifennodd ei mynaich Fucheddau’r saint a ddaeth o Brydain, yn eu plith Buchedd Pol Aurelian gan Wrmonoc. Mae Gwenole yn un o’r anwylaf o saint Llydaw, a chedwir cerflun ohono a gerfiwyd yn 1522 yn ei abaty o hyd.",
    "Perthynai ei deulu i ddwy lan y môr. Yn nhraddodiad Cymru roedd ei fam, Gwen Teirbron, hefyd yn fam i Gadfan, a ddaeth o Lydaw i sefydlu Tywyn a’r fynachlog ar Ynys Enlli. Yng Nghernyw Gwenole yw sant Gunwalloe a Landewednack, ac yng Nghymru ef biau dau gapel coll ac efallai eglwysi Sain Twynnell yn Sir Benfro a Llanwarw yng Ngwent."]),
]
